    <Interaction elementId="RXN-11277">
      <Xref identifier="EC-3.6.1.75" dataSource="Enzyme Nomenclature" />
      <Waypoints>
        <Point elementId="RXN-11277_start" x="545.0" y="1140" elementRef="DIACYLGLYCEROL-PYROPHOSPHATE" relX="1.0" relY="0.0" />
        <Point elementId="RXN-11277_end" arrowHead="Conversion" x="305.0" y="1140" elementRef="L-PHOSPHATIDATE" relX="-1.0" relY="0.0" />
        <Anchor elementId="RXN-11277_anchor" position="0.5" shapeType="Circle" />
      </Waypoints>
      <Graphics lineColor="000000" lineStyle="Solid" lineWidth="1.0" connectorType="Straight" />
      <Comment source="BioCyc">The bifunctional enzyme catalyzes the dephosphorylation of diacylglycerol diphosphate to phosphatidate and the subsequent dephosphorylation of phosphatidate to diacylglycerol (cf. phosphatidate phosphatase |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDATE-PHOSPHATASE-RXN &quot;EC 3.1.3.4&quot;|). It regulates intracellular levels of diacylglycerol diphosphate and phosphatidate, phospholipid molecules believed to play a signaling role in stress response |CITS: [11139591]|.The diacylglycerol pyrophosphate phosphatase activity is induced by zinc depletion, by inositol supplementation, and when cells enter the stationary phase |CITS: [14642771]|. The phosphatase activity of the bifunctional enzyme is Mg2+-independent and N-ethylmaleimide-insensitive and is distinct from the Mg2+-dependent and N-ethylmaleimide-sensitive enzyme |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDATE-PHOSPHATASE-RXN &quot;EC 3.1.3.4&quot;| (phosphatidate phosphatase) |CITS: [9370315]|.The diacylglycerol pyrophosphate phosphatase activity in Saccharomyces cerevisiae is induced by zinc depletion, by inositol supplementation, and when cells enter the stationary phase |CITS: [14642771]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="RXN-11277" />
